 "standard_text": "Find squares, cubes, square roots and cube roots of numbers; use prime factors to find square roots; estimate square roots. (summary of the NERDC JSS 2 Mathematics curriculum)",
 "meta_desc": "Free JSS 2 maths worksheets on squares and square roots, cubes and cube roots, index notation and square roots by prime factors. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "√ is the inverse of squaring",
 "key_sub": "Pair the prime factors for √.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>A square number is n²; a cube number is n³.</li><li>The square root of 196 is 14 because 14² = 196. Every positive number has two square roots, +14 and −14.</li><li>Use prime factors: 576 = 2⁶ × 3², so √576 = 2³ × 3 = 24.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>√1,296</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2⁴ × 3⁴ → 2² × 3²</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 36</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Halving for a square root</td><td>√64 = 8.</td></tr><tr><td>Writing 3³ = 9</td><td>3³ = 27.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting the negative root</td><td>x² = 25 gives x = 5 or x = −5.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
